%I A401143 #8 Oct 20 2026 22:58:00
%S A401143 1,2,5,4,3,5,7,4,6,3,8,5,2,7,4,3,9,6,3,3,8,8,5,3,3,2,10,13,4,3,3,6,9,
%T A401143 9,12,6,6,4,3,5,3,8,11,11,5,5,8,11,3,4,8,5,10,10,10,13,7,4,7,10,4,4,
%U A401143 10,7,4,9,12,12,9,12,15,6,6,6,9,12,3,4,9,6,9,3,6,11,11,11,8,11,14,5
%N A401143 Number of steps starting from 2*n-1 until the first repeated value under the map k -> (5*k+3)/2^v_2(5*k+3) if k == 1 (mod 4) and k -> (5*k+1)/2^v_2(5*k+1) if k == 3 (mod 4) where v_2(k) = A007814(k); or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A401143 a(n) is the number of iterations of T, starting from 2*n - 1, until a value first repeats within that trajectory. For positive odd k, T(k) = (5*k+3)/2^v_2(5*k+3) if k == 1 (mod 4), and T(k) = (5*k+1)/2^v_2(5*k+1) if k == 3 (mod 4), where v_2(m) = A007814(m). In both cases v_2 >= 2.
%C A401143 This map belongs to the same residue-dependent reduced 5k+c family as A400174; the branch for k == 1 (mod 4) uses +3 here, instead of -1 in A400174.
%C A401143 The four directly verified cycles are the fixed point 1 and the three-cycles 31 -> 39 -> 49 -> 31, 37 -> 47 -> 59 -> 37, and 61 -> 77 -> 97 -> 61.
%C A401143 If a trajectory has preperiod length mu and eventual period lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda. A start at 1 has value 1; a start on any of the three-cycles has value 3.
%H A401143 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="/A401143/b401143.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401143 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22726453">The Reduced 5k + 1(3)/4 Collatz-Type Domain with Four Observed Attractors: Algebraic Structure, Basin Statistics, Probabilistic Contraction, and Exhaustive Verification up to 10^10</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A401143 For n = 1, b(1) = 1 and the orbit begins 1, 1. The first repeated value occurs after 1 iteration, so a(1) = 1.
%e A401143 For n = 2, b(2) = 3 and the orbit begins 3, 1, 1. The first repeated value occurs after 2 iterations, so a(2) = 2.
%e A401143 For n = 16, b(16) = 31 and the orbit begins 31, 39, 49, 31. The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(16) = 3.
%e A401143 For n = 19, b(19) = 37 and the orbit begins 37, 47, 59, 37. The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(19) = 3.
%o A401143 (Python)
%o A401143 def T(k):
%o A401143     y = 5*k + (3 if k % 4 == 1 else 1)
%o A401143     while y % 2 == 0:
%o A401143         y //= 2
%o A401143     return y
%o A401143 def a(n):
%o A401143     k, seen = 2*n - 1, set()
%o A401143     while k not in seen:
%o A401143         seen.add(k)
%o A401143         k = T(k)
%o A401143     return len(seen)
%o A401143 # This program assumes that the trajectory eventually repeats; it does not decide divergence.
%Y A401143 Cf. A005408, A007814, A400174, A399120, A399761.
%K A401143 nonn,new
%O A401143 1,2
%A A401143 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Oct 16 2026
